 following expressions all mean \"two times $y$\":\n$2y,\\qquad 2 \\cdot y,\\qquad 2 \\times y$.\n\nWhen a number is next to a variable, it means that we multiply them together. We can also represent multiplication using the dot symbol ($\\cdot$) or the familiar \"times\" symbol ($\\times$), though we tend to avoid using the ($\\times$) symbol because it looks a lot like the variable $x$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180773 #edb/ref 1065 8167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180773 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dfb2834b-8f6f-55c4-b933-6f80cbf9e842" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180773 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180774 #edb/ref 1064 "The **quotient** of two numbers means that we need to divide the first number by the second number.\n\nFor example, \"the quotient of $x$ and two\" means the same thing as \"$x$ divided by two\" and can be written as either expression below:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{2},\\qquad x \\div 2 \\,\n$$\n\nIn algebra, we prefer to represent division using the fraction bar instead of the ($\\div$) symbol." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180774 #edb/ref 1065 8168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180774 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "966707d2-a5e7-5ce1-a0b4-1c2991c80e3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180774 #edb/ref 1067 "The Division Sign in Algebra" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
